.TH TEST 1 
.SH NAME
test \-  condition command
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B test
expr
.PP
.B [
expr
.B ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I test
evaluates the expression
.IR expr ,
and if its value is true then returns zero exit status; otherwise, a
non zero exit status is returned.
.I test
returns a non zero exit if there are no arguments.
.PP
.B Note:
v7's page gave only the first of the two SYNOPSIS lines, although the program has
always accepted both.
.B "/bin/[ is /bin/test"
\- one inode with two names, and the only hard link on this system's root filesystem.
Entered by that name the program requires a closing
.BR ] ,
and complains
.B "test: ] missing"
if it is absent.
.PP
The following primitives are used to construct
.IR expr .
.TP 9n
.BR \-r " file"
true if the file exists and is readable.
.TP 
.BR \-w " file"
true if the file exists and is writable.
.PP
.B Note:
those two are answered by opening the file and closing it again rather than by
.IR access (2),
so they report on the
.I effective
user and not the real one.  That is v7's implementation, and it is kept.  It matters here
because every shell on this system is the super-user's, which can open anything:
.B \-w
is true of a file with no write permission at all.
.TP 9n
.BR \-f " file"
true if the file exists and is not a directory.
.TP 
.BR \-d " file"
true if the file exists and is a directory.
.TP 
.BR \-s " file"
true if the file exists and has a size greater than zero.
.TP 
.BR \-t " [ fildes ]"
true if the open file whose file descriptor number is
.I fildes
(1 by default)
is associated with a terminal device.
.TP 
.BR \-z " s1"
true if the length of string
.I s1
is zero.
.TP 
.BR \-n " s1"
true if the length of the string
.I s1
is nonzero.
.TP 
.RB s1 " = " s2
true
if the strings
.I s1
and
.I s2
are equal.
.TP 
.RB s1 " != " s2
true
if the strings
.I s1
and
.I s2
are not equal.
.TP
s1
true if
.I s1
is not the null string.
.TP
.BR \-l " s1"
the length of the string
.IR s1 ,
for use on the left of one of the integer comparisons below \-
.B "test \-l abcde \-eq 5"
is true.
.B Note:
v7's page did not document this primary, although the program has always had it.
.TP
.RB n1 " \-eq " n2
true if the integers
.I n1
and
.I n2
are algebraically equal.
Any of the comparisons
.BR \-ne ,
.BR \-gt ,
.BR \-ge ,
.BR \-lt ,
or
.BR \-le
may be used in place of
.BR \-eq .
.PP
These primaries may be combined with the
following operators:
.TP 
.B  !
unary negation operator
.TP 
.B  \-a
binary
.I and
operator
.TP 
.B  \-o
binary
.I or
operator
.TP 
.BR "( " "expr" " )"
parentheses for grouping.
.PP
.B \-a
has higher precedence than
.B \-o.
Notice that all the operators and flags are separate
arguments to
.IR test .
Notice also that parentheses are meaningful
to the Shell and must be escaped.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
An expression that does not parse produces one of
.BR "test: argument expected" ,
.BR "test: ) expected" ,
.B "test: ] missing"
or
.BR "test: unknown operator " x
on the diagnostic output, and an exit status of 255.
.PP
.B Note:
.B "no caller on this machine can see that 255."
A wait status is the exit code shifted left by eight and it is returned through r12, a
fifteen-bit index register, so every code from 128 up arrives truncated: the Shell's
.B $?
reads 127.  See
.IR wait (2).
Exit statuses 0 and 1 \- the answers this program exists to give \- are unaffected.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
sh(1), find(1), wait(2)
